        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "05520815-dd95-534f-9fe0-8991bcb0b0a8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Dziga Vertov, Frederic Zelnik, Lya Mara, Fred Louis Lerch, Stewart Rome\n- Films: Lullaby (1937 film), The Crimson Circle (1929 film)\n- Roles: documentary film director, newsreel director, cinema theorist, producer-director, actor\n- Topics: Soviet documentary film, German silent cinema, British-German crime film\n\nFacts:\n- Frederic Zelnik was born on 17 May 1885 and died on 29 November 1950.\n- Dziga Vertov was born David Abelevich Kaufman, was also known as Denis Kaufman, and lived from 2 January 1896 to 12 February 1954.\n- Frederic Zelnik was one of the most important producer-directors of German silent cinema.\n- Frederic Zelnik also appeared on screen as an actor.\n- The Crimson Circle is a 1929 British-German crime film directed by Frederic Zelnik and starring Lya Mara, Fred Louis Lerch, and Stewart Rome.\n- Dziga Vertov was a Soviet pioneer documentary film director, newsreel director, and cinema theorist.\n- Lullaby is a 1937 Soviet documentary film directed by Dziga Vertov.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "32231256-c542-5eff-b220-edce0b4a78b8",
        "text": "Page: Dziga Vertov. Sentence: Dziga Vertov (Russian: Дзига Вертов ; born David Abelevich Kaufman, Russian: Дави́д А́белевич Ка́уфман , and also known as Denis Kaufman; 2 January 1896 – 12 February 1954) was a Soviet pioneer documentary film and newsreel director, as well as a cinema theorist.\nPage: Frederic Zelnik. Sentence: Frederic Zelnik (17 May 1885 - 29 November 1950) was one of the most important producers-directors of the German silent cinema.\nPage: Frederic Zelnik. Sentence: He also appeared on screen as an actor.\nPage: Lullaby (1937 film). Sentence: \"Kolybelnaya\") is a 1937 Soviet documentary film directed by Dziga Vertov.\nPage: The Crimson Circle (1929 film). Sentence: The Crimson Circle (German: Der rote Kreis) is a 1929 British-German crime film directed by Frederic Zelnik and starring Lya Mara, Fred Louis Lerch, and Stewart Rome.",
